Subject: Corrected BIC
Message-ID: <Lfrxcs-HCAE.ZwMn6wNKj2IJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/Lfrxcs-HCAE/m/ZwMn6wNKj2IJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

 Dear lavaan users,
                             we conducted a longitudinal study in which data was collected for six occasions and latent growth curve (LGC) model using lavaan was fit to the data. For some reason, we need to fit LGC for first five occasions using same longitudinal data subjects. We have a situation where we want to select model on the basis of corrected BIC (CBIC). We know that model with lower CBIC should be preferred.  My question is, whether these two LGC models (Six occasions vs five occasions) be compared and selection of model with lower CBIC can be made?

 any suggestions or pointer to relevant literature will be highly appreciated.
 Thank you in advance !

From u89c9fc822e34b78c at groups.google.com  Sun Jul 28 12:45:40 2013
From: u89c9fc822e34b78c at groups.google.com (Yves Rosseel)
Date: Sun, 28 Jul 2013 12:45:40 +0000
Subject: Re: Lower standard error when regression coefficients are constrained to be equal
Message-ID: <W2XHNEUffAM.whDoXAuVJfcJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <W2XHNEUffAM.H1q1wGcHnF8J@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <W2XHNEUffAM.H1q1wGcHnF8J@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/W2XHNEUffAM/m/whDoXAuVJfcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> I see that the unstandardized regression coefficient is roughly the same
> from model 1 to model 2 but the standard error has decreased in model 2
> (also resulting in a drop in p). My question is: what has caused this
> drop in standard error in model 2?

More information.

Try

zapsmall( diag(lavaan:::computeExpectedInformation(fit1@Model,
samplestats=fit1@SampleStats)) )

and

zapsmall( diag(lavaan:::computeExpectedInformation(fit2@Model,
samplestats=fit2@SampleStats)) )

to get a rough idea of the 'information' per parameter (ignoring all
off-diagonal elements)

Then, compare to

zapsmall( diag(vcov(fit1)) )

and

zapsmall( diag(vcov(fit2)) )

The 'information' contained in the three separate regression
coefficients is 'added up'. So you got about 3x more information. The
variance-covariance matrix of the parameters is the inverse of the
information matrix, and the standard errors are its square-root diagonal
elements. More information, smaller standard errors.

Hope this helps,

Yves.

